# Source list for requirements-lock.txt
# (pip-compile --generate-hashes --no-annotate --no-header requirements.in).
# This is the dependency surface environment.yml's `pip:` block lists directly;
# environment.yml just points at the compiled lock (`-r requirements-lock.txt`).
# Conda-managed dependencies (python, pytest, ...) are not here — they are pinned
# directly in environment.yml. See docs/standards/dependencies.md.
#
# requirements-lock.txt is NOT included in this reference example — a committed lock is
# a real, resolved snapshot that goes stale the moment you touch this file, so shipping a
# frozen one here would be misleading (and it's never meant to be hand-preserved anyway —
# see "To update" below). Generate your own first one before running `conda env create`:
#   pip install pip-tools
#   pip-compile --generate-hashes --no-annotate --no-header requirements.in
# then commit the result. Regenerate it the same way every time you edit this file.
#
# To update: edit a range here, then re-run
#   pip-compile --generate-hashes --no-annotate --no-header requirements.in
# and commit both files together. CI fails if requirements-lock.txt drifts from this file.
# Prefer letting CI's own recompile (Linux, the actual deploy target) be authoritative over
# a local Windows recompile — Windows and Linux can silently resolve different transitive
# packages for the same requirements.in (see the example comment below); if CI's drift
# check fails after a local Windows edit, trust CI's diff over your own local output.

# Example of the platform-conditional-package gotcha this project hit twice (colorama via
# tqdm's Windows-only marker; tzdata via a pandas version that needed it on Windows but not
# Linux): when a package silently differs between a local Windows compile and CI's Linux
# compile, pin it here explicitly and unconditionally rather than letting a transitive
# marker decide it per platform. Delete this comment and the example line below once you
# have real dependencies and have hit (or ruled out) the same issue yourself.
# example-transitive-package==1.2.3

# Ruff is pinned exactly — its formatter/lint output changes between minor versions. Keep
# it on the same version as `rev` in .pre-commit-config.yaml.
ruff==0.16.6
pre-commit>=4.6.2,<5
